---
import Cite from '../../components/Cite.astro';
import Article from '../../layouts/Article.astro';
import { codexPage } from '../../data/pages.js';
import { federationRows, matchRows } from '../../lib/federation.js';

const page = codexPage('one-third-rule');
const federations = federationRows(4, 10);
const matches = matchRows(5, 9);
---
<Article slug={page.slug}>
  <p>Two numbers describe every federation of <em>n</em> guardians:</p>
  <p class="formula">f = ⌊(n − 1) / 3⌋ &nbsp;faulty guardians it can tolerate<br />t = n − f &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;guardians that must agree before it acts</p>
  <p>
    Fedimint’s own code defines exactly these two values, as <code>max_evil</code> and <code>threshold</code>.<Cite page={page} s="fmNumPeers" />
  </p>

  <h2>Why a third?</h2>
  <p>
    A guardian cannot wait to hear from everyone, because up to <em>f</em> of them may never answer. So it acts after hearing from
    <em>n</em> − <em>f</em>. But up to <em>f</em> of those answers may be lies. For the honest answers to outnumber the lies in that
    group, it needs <em>n</em> − 2<em>f</em> &gt; <em>f</em>, which is <em>n</em> &gt; 3<em>f</em>.<Cite page={page} s="lsp" /><Cite page={page} s="pbft" />
  </p>
  <p>
    There is a second way to see it. Any two groups of <em>n</em> − <em>f</em> guardians overlap in at least <em>n</em> − 2<em>f</em>
    guardians, which is at least <em>f</em> + 1 when <em>n</em> ≥ 3<em>f</em> + 1. So every two quorums share at least one honest
    guardian, and an honest guardian never vouches for two conflicting decisions.
  </p>

  <figure>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <caption>Faults tolerated and signatures needed, from the formulas above</caption>
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Guardians (n)</th><th scope="col">Faulty tolerated (f)</th><th scope="col">Signatures needed (t)</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {federations.map((row) => <tr><th scope="row">{row.guardians}</th><td>{row.tolerated}</td><td>{row.threshold}</td></tr>)}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <figcaption>Adding a guardian only raises the number of faults tolerated at 4, 7, 10 and every third size after that.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h2>The rule in a match</h2>
  <p>
    The game uses the same arithmetic. The number of Byzantine guardians is the most the federation can tolerate, and a forged
    withdrawal needs as many signatures as a real one. The federation halts, and the Byzantines win, as soon as they make up a third
    or more of the guardians still seated.
  </p>
  <figure>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <caption>Each match size in Byzantine Council</caption>
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Players</th><th scope="col">Byzantine</th><th scope="col">Signatures needed</th><th scope="col">Honest guardians you can wrongly rotate out</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {matches.map((row) => <tr><th scope="row">{row.guardians}</th><td>{row.tolerated}</td><td>{row.threshold}</td><td>{row.survivable}</td></tr>)}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <figcaption>
      With 5 players and 1 Byzantine, the federation survives one wrong rotation (1 of 4 is a quarter). A second leaves 1 of 3, a
      third, and the federation halts.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>
</Article>
